The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
err = request_firmware(&fw, bts_scr_name, &lldev->serdev->dev);
if (err || !fw->data || !fw->size) {
    bt_dev_err(lldev->hu.hdev, "request_firmware failed(errno %d) for %s",
               err, bts_scr_name);
    return -EINVAL;
}
```
Reason:
When `request_firmware()` succeeds (`err == 0`) but the firmware content is invalid (`!fw->data` or `!fw->size`), the code enters the error path and returns `-EINVAL` without calling `release_firmware(fw)`. This causes a resource leak because the firmware data allocated by `request_firmware()` is never freed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (err || !fw->data || !fw->size) {
    bt_dev_err(lldev->hu.hdev, "request_firmware failed(errno %d) for %s",
               err, bts_scr_name);
    if (!err)
        release_firmware(fw);
    return -EINVAL;
}
```
Reason:
Adding `if (!err) release_firmware(fw);` ensures that the firmware is freed before returning when `request_firmware()` succeeded but the firmware data is invalid. This closes the leak while still properly reporting the error and not attempting to release firmware that was never loaded (when `err` is non‑zero, `fw` is NULL and should not be released).

